      "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Consensus identifies 'meat' primarily as advanced or mature spiritual teaching—deeper doctrine for spiritually grown believers—contrasted with 'milk' as elementary instruction (1 Cor 3:1-2; Heb 5:12-14). Common listed alternatives include doing God's will as spiritual nourishment (John 4:32-34), Christ's flesh as true food (John 6:55), and meat as an emblem of Christian liberty/conscience.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book defines meat as 'the word obeyed to completeness': milk is the word merely received, and meat is that same word carried through to complete obedience, which exercises the senses to discern (fusing John 4:34's 'my meat is to do the will' with Heb 5:14's 'by reason of use have their senses exercised').\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Meat = a more advanced grade of spiritual teaching/doctrine, distinguished from milk by depth of content and suited to the mature.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Meat = the same word of God, distinguished from milk not by content but by being fully obeyed/practiced, with obedience producing discernment.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book relocates the milk/meat boundary from content-depth to obedience. Consensus admits 'meat' as deeper doctrinal content one can receive; the book excludes doctrine-not-yet-obeyed from counting as meat and insists milk and meat are 'the same word,' denying that meat is a distinct advanced body of teaching. It reclassifies the maturity passages (Heb 5:14) as being about practiced obedience rather than doctrinal sophistication, and promotes the consensus's 'doing God's will' alternative (John 4:34) into the defining criterion.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"Partial: the consensus primary implies meat differs from milk in content (deeper doctrine); the book asserts the content is identical and only the degree of obedience differs. However, both readings keep the same referent (the word of God as nourishment) and the book's obedience axis is itself a listed common consensus alternative, so this is boundary disagreement within a shared category rather than replacement of the referent.\",\"rationale\":\"The book does not abandon the consensus category—the word of God as spiritual food in a milk-to-meat progression—but it materially changes the condition that makes something 'meat.' Consensus: advanced content; Book: complete obedience to the same content. This synthesizes the consensus primary (milk/meat maturity, Heb 5:14) with a common consensus alternative (John 4:34, meat as doing God's will), yielding a genuinely different boundary but a recognizably related core. Because the central referent survives and the obedience reading is already present in consensus as an alternative, this is REFINED rather than DIVERGENT or NOVEL.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"One could argue DIVERGENT: the consensus core says meat is teaching that differs in content from milk, while the book flatly says it is 'the same word,' which cannot both be true of the milk/meat contrast in the same sense—accepting the book's account requires rejecting the consensus's content-based identification of what 'strong meat' is in Heb 5 and 1 Cor 3.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
    }
